{"maxId":305,"entries":{"chapter-51-study-osteoporosis|Chapter 51 Study: Osteoporosis":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"copy|DXA T-score ≥−1.0 is normal, below −1.0 but above −2.5 is low bone mass, and ≤−2.5 is osteoporosis. A T-score ≤−2.5 plus a fragility fracture defines severe/established osteoporosis in the WHO classification (Table 51-1, p760; Bone Health and Osteoporosis Foundation).":["u3"],"copy|Each 1-SD drop in T-score ≈ doubles fracture risk (p759).":["u4"],"copy|Postmenopausal osteoporosis: women 51–75 years, driven by estrogen loss. Senile osteoporosis: >60 years, both sexes, from reduced bone turnover/osteoblast numbers (p759).":["u5"],"copy|54 million Americans at risk of fracture; ~10 million have osteoporosis, 43 million have low bone mass (NOF 2018) (p760).":["u6"],"copy|Women: >250,000 hip + >500,000 spine fractures/year. Men: 250,000 fractures/year, 75,000 hip (p760).":["u7"],"copy|By age 90: 1/3 of women, 1/6 of men will have a hip fracture; lifetime vertebral fracture risk 12% in both sexes (p760).":["u8"],"copy|20%–25% mortality in the year after hip fracture; ~50% of women do not fully recover prior function (p760).":["u9"],"copy|Annual US cost >$22 billion; projected $25 billion/3 million fractures by 2025 (p760).":["u10"],"copy|Cortical bone accounts for approximately 80% of total adult skeletal mass and trabecular bone for approximately 20%. Trabecular bone has higher metabolic activity and is prominent in the vertebrae and pelvis (p760; WHO Technical Report Series 921, correcting the mass attribution).":["u11"],"copy|Bone remodeling cycle (basic multicellular unit) lasts 6–9 months (p763).":["u12"],"copy|Menopause: rapid spine bone loss ~3%/year for ~5 years, then >0.5%/year generalized (p765).":["u13"],"copy|Age-related decline ~0.5%/year after the 3rd decade; marrow fat can occupy up to 90% of marrow cavity in older adults (p766).":["u14"],"copy|Osteosarcopenia: the term for concurrent osteopenia/osteoporosis + sarcopenia, coined from muscle-bone cross-talk research (p767).":["u15"],"copy|Men: testosterone declines ~1.2%/year with aging (p767).":["u16"],"copy|Estrogen & androgen both inhibit bone resorption and stimulate bone formation (Table 51-2 lists them under stimulators of formation and inhibitors of resorption); vitamin D is listed as a stimulator of both resorption and formation (as are fibroblast growth factors and platelet-derived growth factor) (Table 51-2, p764).":["u17"],"copy|As many as 1/3 or more of those with \"osteopenia\" on a plain x-ray may have a T-score worse than −2.5, and as many as half will be in the −2.5 to −1.0 range — so x-ray \"osteopenia\" is an indication for BMD measurement (p768).":["u18"],"copy|In one study, 3/4 of postmenopausal women with a distal radius fracture were undiagnosed or untreated; as many as 50% of women with a hip fracture leave hospital without treatment (p768).":["u19"],"copy|Repeat fracture risk in the first year after a fracture: 20% (p768).":["u20"],"copy|Key clinical risk factors: age (the single most critical contributor), current smoking, body weight < 127 lb, corticosteroid use > 3 months, prior fracture, family history (Table 51-4) (p769).":["u21"],"copy|FRAX: estimates 10-year absolute fracture risk; major limitation — does not include falls (p770).":["u22"],"copy|Garvan tool: includes history of previous falls; gives 5- and 10-year risk (p770).":["u23"],"copy|Figure 51-7 algorithm: combines fall/fracture/age/SARC-F screening with DXA + ALM to jointly diagnose osteoporosis and sarcopenia (p770).":["u24"],"copy|SARC-F score ≥ 4 predicts clinically important sarcopenia (~95% specificity) (p771).":["u25"],"copy|Fall-risk red flags: can't hold a semi-tandem stand 10 seconds; gait speed < 0.8 m/s; Timed Up and Go > 14 seconds (p771).":["u26"],"copy|DXA is preferred; in >200,000 postmenopausal women screened in primary care, ~50% had previously undetected low BMD (7% osteoporosis); BMD screening reduced hip-fracture incidence by 36% in another study (p771).":["u27"],"copy|SOF cohort: 54% of women who suffered a hip fracture had a hip T-score better than −2.5 at baseline — BMD alone under-captures risk; age matters more (p771).":["u28"],"copy|10-year fracture probability in an 80-year-old is >2× that of a 50-year-old at the same T-score (p771).":["u29"],"copy|Secondary-cause workup: primary hyperparathyroidism → ionized calcium + PTH; Paget disease → alkaline phosphatase; glucocorticoid use → medical history (Table 51-3, p769).":["u30"],"copy|Hazzard discusses total elemental calcium 1,200–1,500 mg/day and vitamin D at least 800 IU/day, sometimes 2,000 IU/day or more to reach a bone-disease target of 75 nmol/L (30 ng/mL) (p772). Current BHOF calcium targets from diet plus supplements are 1,200 mg/day for women ≥51 and men ≥71 and 1,000 mg/day for men 50–70. Individualize supplementation and avoid exceeding the total target through unnecessary tablets (BHOF calcium and vitamin D).":["u31"],"copy|High-dose vitamin D bolus dosing (500,000 IU/year or 60,000 IU/month) increases fall risk — avoid (p773).":["u32"],"copy|Frank deficiency (≤30 nmol/L): loading dose 50,000 IU cholecalciferol or ergocalciferol; all others: daily 1000–4000 IU cholecalciferol (p773).":["u33"],"copy|Alendronate 70 mg orally weekly reduced hip and vertebral fracture risk by roughly 50% over three years in the studies summarized (Table 51-5, p774). The cited age subgroups ≤75 and >75 had 51% and 38% lower vertebral fracture risk at 12 months, respectively. Estimated time to benefit in those ≥70 was 8 months (p775). US labeling does not recommend alendronate at creatinine clearance <35 mL/min. Contraindications to oral use include delayed esophageal emptying, inability to remain upright for 30 minutes and hypocalcemia (FDA Fosamax prescribing information).":["u34"],"copy|Risedronate 35 mg weekly PO (or 75 mg × 2 consecutive days/month, or 150 mg monthly PO): 41%–49% ↓ vertebral, 36% ↓ nonvertebral fracture over 3 years; also approved for glucocorticoid-induced osteoporosis (Table 51-5, p774).":["u35"],"copy|Zoledronic acid 5 mg IV yearly for three years reduced vertebral, nonvertebral and hip fracture risk by 70%, 25% and 41%, respectively, in the postmenopausal osteoporosis trial (p775). A separate trial in patients treated after hip-fracture repair reported deaths in 9.6% with zoledronic acid versus 13.3% with placebo (p776; HORIZON Recurrent Fracture Trial). US labeling contraindicates the osteoporosis infusion in acute renal impairment or creatinine clearance <35 mL/min.":["u36"],"copy|Denosumab 60 mg SC every 6 months (dose per Table 51-5, p774 — the chapter's own prose on p776 prints \"60 μg,\" a book table-vs-text discrepancy, see chapter note): 40% ↓ hip, 68% ↓ vertebral, 20% ↓ nonvertebral fracture; antifracture effect declines to pretreatment levels 12 months after stopping, and fracture risk rises markedly in those with prior vertebral fracture (p776). Plan antiresorptive follow-on before stopping or delaying treatment. Advanced CKD, particularly dialysis or CKD–mineral and bone disorder, raises the risk of severe hypocalcemia (FDA boxed warning).":["u37"],"copy|Teriparatide 20 micrograms SC daily reduced vertebral and nonvertebral fractures by 65% and 53%, respectively, in the cited trial (pp774,776). US labeling permits treatment beyond two years only if high fracture risk persists or returns. Avoid in people at increased baseline osteosarcoma risk, including Paget disease, bone metastases, prior skeletal malignancy or radiation involving the skeleton. Avoid in underlying hypercalcemic disorders as a separate precaution (FDA Forteo prescribing information).":["u38"],"copy|Abaloparatide 80 micrograms SC daily reduced new vertebral fractures by 86% and nonvertebral fractures by 43% in the overall 18-month trial. US labeling does not recommend use for more than two years in a lifetime (p777; FDA Tymlos prescribing information).":["u39"],"copy|Romosozumab 210 mg SC monthly for 12 doses. In FRAME, romosozumab versus placebo for one year, followed by denosumab in both groups, reduced new vertebral fractures 75% at two years. In ARCH, romosozumab versus alendronate for one year, followed by alendronate in both groups, reduced vertebral, nonvertebral and hip fractures by 48%, 19% and 38%, respectively (p777). US labeling advises against starting within one year of MI or stroke and calls for individual cardiovascular risk assessment (FDA Evenity boxed warning).":["u40"],"copy|Teriparatide and abaloparatide should be considered as starting therapy for very high fracture risk and/or multiple fractures (p777); after teriparatide, treatment with an antiresorptive is imperative to maintain BMD gains (p776).":["u41"],"copy|ONJ: very rare at osteoporosis dosing, 0.001%–0.01% (p776); in postmenopausal women on oral bisphosphonates specifically, 0.02%–0.06% — a dental check should not delay treatment (p777).":["u42"],"copy|AFF: 3.2–50 per 100,000 person-years on bisphosphonates; >100 per 100,000 with long-term use (p777); new groin or thigh pain warrants femur imaging for an incomplete atypical fracture. Prodromal pain can precede a complete fracture by weeks or months (pp776–777; FDA bisphosphonate prescribing information).":["u43"],"copy|All residents: vitamin D ≥800 IU/day + total calcium 1200 mg/day from food and supplements as needed. Individualize in CKD or hypercalcemia and check 25(OH)D first (p778).":["u44"],"copy|If 25(OH)D ≤50 nmol/L → 2000 IU/day; if ≤30 nmol/L → loading dose 50,000 IU, then 3000–4000 IU/day (p778).":["u45"],"copy|Bisphosphonates are first-line (IV favors adherence); denosumab an equal alternative given SC route (p778).":["u46"],"recall|Recall":["u47"],"copy|CHAPTER 51s / HAZZARD":["u48"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 51; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u49"],"copy|Definitions (WHO criteria, Table 51-1)":["u50"],"copy|Epidemiology":["u51"],"copy|Bone biology (brief)":["u52"],"copy|Presentation":["u53"],"copy|Risk assessment":["u54"],"copy|BMD & diagnosis":["u55"],"copy|Nutrition":["u56"],"copy|Pharmacologic treatment (Table 51-5)":["u57"],"copy|Complications":["u58"],"copy|Nursing home / long-term care":["u59"],"copy|1.":["u60"],"copy|What T-score defines osteoporosis per WHO criteria, and what additionally defines \"severe\" osteoporosis?":["u61"],"copy|T-score ≤ −2.5 defines osteoporosis; severe osteoporosis is a T-score ≤ −2.5 plus a fragility fracture (p760).":["u62"],"copy|3.":["u63"],"copy|What proportion of women and men will suffer a hip fracture by age 90, and what is the lifetime vertebral fracture risk?":["u64"],"copy|One-third of women and one-sixth of men will have a hip fracture by age 90; lifetime vertebral fracture risk is about 12% and similar in both sexes (p760).":["u65"],"copy|4.":["u66"],"copy|What is a major limitation of the FRAX tool, and which tool addresses it?":["u67"],"copy|FRAX does not include falls as a risk factor, which can underestimate risk in frequent fallers; the Garvan tool includes a history of previous falls and reports risk at both 5 and 10 years (p770).":["u68"],"copy|5.":["u69"],"copy|What SARC-F score threshold predicts clinically important sarcopenia, and what does the score assess?":["u70"],"copy|A score ≥ 4 (out of 10) is predictive; SARC-F assesses Strength, Assistance with walking, Rising from a chair, Climbing stairs, and Falls (p771).":["u71"],"copy|6.":["u72"],"copy|Name three office-based fall-risk screens and their positive thresholds mentioned in the chapter.":["u73"],"copy|Inability to hold a semi-tandem stand for 10 seconds; gait velocity < 0.8 m/s; Timed Up and Go > 14 seconds (also one-leg stand < 5 seconds and POMA score < 19) (p771).":["u74"],"copy|7.":["u75"],"copy|In the Study of Osteoporotic Fractures cohort, what fraction of women who suffered a hip fracture actually had a T-score better than −2.5 — and what does this imply?":["u76"],"copy|54% — so most of these hip fractures occurred above the osteoporosis threshold; BMD is not the fundamental determinant of fracture risk, and clinical risk factors (especially age) must be assessed alongside BMD (p771).":["u77"],"copy|8.":["u78"],"copy|What are the daily calcium and vitamin D targets for older adults, and what serum 25(OH)D level is the therapeutic goal?":["u79"],"copy|Hazzard’s osteoporosis regimen discusses elemental calcium 1,200–1,500 mg/day and vitamin D at least 800 IU/day, sometimes 2,000 IU/day or more for its 75 nmol/L bone-disease target (p772; p767 separately mentions above 80 nmol/L).":["u80"],"copy|BHOF advises 1,200 mg/day total calcium for women ≥51 and men ≥71, and 1,000 mg/day for men 50–70.":["u80"],"copy|A universal 25(OH)D target for preventing falls in otherwise healthy adults has not been established (BHOF Clinician’s Guide; Endocrine Society 2024).":["u80"],"copy|10.":["u81"],"copy|What are the approximate fracture-risk reductions with zoledronic acid 5 mg IV yearly, and what other major benefit did it show?":["u82"],"copy|In the postmenopausal osteoporosis trial, vertebral, nonvertebral and hip fractures fell 70%, 25% and 41%.":["u83"],"copy|In a separate post-hip-fracture trial, all-cause mortality was 9.6% versus 13.3% with placebo (pp775–776; PMID 17878149).":["u83"],"copy|12.":["u84"],"copy|What is the maximum treatment duration for teriparatide, abaloparatide, and romosozumab, and why?":["u85"],"copy|US labeling permits teriparatide beyond two years only when high fracture risk persists or returns.":["u86"],"copy|Abaloparatide beyond two years in a lifetime is not recommended.":["u86"],"copy|Romosozumab is limited to 12 monthly doses as the anabolic effect wanes, followed by an antiresorptive when indicated.":["u87"],"copy|Its separate boxed warning advises against starting within one year of MI or stroke (pp776–777; FDA Forteo, Tymlos and Evenity labels).":["u88"],"copy|13.":["u89"],"copy|Compare the estimated incidence of osteonecrosis of the jaw (ONJ) at osteoporosis dosing versus in patients with postmenopausal osteoporosis on oral bisphosphonates specifically.":["u90"],"copy|0.001%–0.01% at recommended osteoporosis dosing generally; 0.02%–0.06% specifically in postmenopausal women on oral bisphosphonates — both very low, and a dental check should not delay starting treatment (p776–777).":["u91"],"copy|14.":["u92"],"copy|What is the absolute risk range of atypical femoral fracture (AFF) with bisphosphonate use, and what symptom should prompt an x-ray?":["u93"],"copy|The reported AFF risk is 3.2–50 per 100,000 person-years, and may exceed 100 per 100,000 after long exposure.":["u94"],"copy|New groin or thigh pain can signal an incomplete fracture weeks or months before a complete fracture.":["u94"],"copy|Obtain femur imaging (pp776–777; FDA bisphosphonate prescribing information).":["u94"],"copy|15.":["u95"],"copy|What are the vitamin D repletion steps for a nursing-home resident found to have 25(OH)D ≤ 30 nmol/L?":["u96"],"copy|A loading dose of 50,000 IU, followed by a maintenance dose of 3000–4000 IU/day (p778).":["u97"],"copy|Answer":["u98","u99","u100","u101","u102","u103","u104","u105","u106","u107","u108","u109"],"copy|Hazzard limits teriparatide and abaloparatide to 2 years because animal studies found osteosarcoma with teriparatide (pp776–777).":["u211"],"copy|It limits romosozumab to 12 months, citing excess cardiovascular events in one phase 3 trial and a concern about possible oncogenesis extrapolated from the teriparatide rodent findings (p777).":["u212"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): the US teriparatide label identifies the osteosarcoma signal in rats and allows consideration of treatment beyond two years only if high fracture risk persists or returns.":["u86"],"copy|Use of abaloparatide beyond two years in a lifetime is not recommended.":["u86"],"copy|Romosozumab is limited to 12 monthly doses because its anabolic effect wanes, followed by an antiresorptive when indicated.":["u87"],"copy|Its boxed warning advises against starting within one year of myocardial infarction or stroke.":["u88"],"copy|Forteo · Tymlos · Evenity.":["u213"],"copy|Table 51-1 on p760 prints normal >−1, osteopenia ≤1 but >−2.5 [sic — the upper bound lacks a minus sign], osteoporosis ≤−2.5, and severe osteoporosis ≤−2.5 plus a fragility fracture.Current practice (not the book): Standard DXA interpretation uses normal ≥−1.0, low bone mass below −1.0 and above −2.5, and osteoporosis ≤−2.5. Bone Health and Osteoporosis Foundation.":["u3"],"copy|Cortical bone predominates in long bones. Hazzard prints that trabecular bone predominates in vertebrae and pelvis and “makes up 80% of skeletal mass” (p760). [sic — adult skeletal mass is approximately 80% cortical and 20% trabecular; WHO Technical Report Series 921.] Trabecular bone has greater metabolic activity.":["u11"],"copy|Hazzard discusses total elemental calcium 1,200–1,500 mg/day and vitamin D at least 800 IU/day, sometimes 2,000 IU/day or more to reach a bone-disease target of 75 nmol/L (30 ng/mL) (p772).Current practice (not the book): Current BHOF calcium targets from diet plus supplements are 1,200 mg/day for women ≥51 and men ≥71 and 1,000 mg/day for men 50–70. Individualize supplementation and avoid exceeding the total target through unnecessary tablets (BHOF calcium and vitamin D).":["u31"],"copy|Zoledronic acid 5 mg IV yearly for three years reduced vertebral, nonvertebral and hip fracture risk by 70%, 25% and 41%, respectively, in the postmenopausal osteoporosis trial (p775). A separate trial in patients treated after hip-fracture repair reported deaths in 9.6% with zoledronic acid versus 13% with placebo (p776).Current practice (not the book): HORIZON reported the unrounded placebo mortality of 13.3% versus 9.6% with zoledronic acid. US labeling contraindicates the osteoporosis infusion in acute renal impairment or CrCl <35 mL/min. HORIZON Recurrent Fracture Trial.":["u36"],"copy|Denosumab 60 mg SC every 6 months (dose per Table 51-5, p774 — the chapter's own prose on p776 prints \"60 μg,\" a book table-vs-text discrepancy, see chapter note): 40% ↓ hip, 68% ↓ vertebral, 20% ↓ nonvertebral fracture; antifracture effect declines to pretreatment levels 12 months after stopping, and fracture risk rises markedly in those with prior vertebral fracture (p776).Current practice (not the book): Plan antiresorptive follow-on before stopping or delaying treatment. Advanced CKD, particularly dialysis or CKD–mineral and bone disorder, raises the risk of severe hypocalcemia (FDA boxed warning).":["u37"],"copy|Table 51-5 lists approximately 57% lower vertebral fracture risk with abaloparatide 80 μg SC daily and a maximum duration of 24 months (p774). [sic — the chapter’s 18-month trial account gives an 86% reduction in new vertebral fractures and 43% in nonvertebral fractures (p777).]Current practice (not the book): US labeling does not recommend treatment for more than two years in a lifetime. Tymlos prescribing information.":["u39"],"copy|Romosozumab 210 mg SC monthly for 12 doses. In FRAME, romosozumab versus placebo for one year, followed by denosumab in both groups, reduced new vertebral fractures 75% at two years. In ARCH, romosozumab versus alendronate for one year, followed by alendronate in both groups, reduced vertebral, nonvertebral and hip fractures by 48%, 19% and 38%, respectively (p777).Current practice (not the book): US labeling advises against starting within one year of MI or stroke and calls for individual cardiovascular risk assessment (FDA Evenity boxed warning). Evenity prescribing information.":["u40"],"copy|AFF: 3.2–50 per 100,000 person-years on bisphosphonates; >100 per 100,000 with long-term use (p777). Hazzard states that fracture precedes pain in most cases. New groin or thigh pain warrants femur radiographs for an atypical femoral fracture; a bone scan or MRI may be needed (pp776–777).Current practice (not the book): FDA bisphosphonate labeling notes that prodromal thigh or groin pain may occur weeks or months before a complete fracture. Alendronate prescribing information.":["u43"],"copy|Hazzard lists fragility fractures of the wrist, vertebrae or hip as sufficient to trigger therapy without BMD testing.":["u214"],"copy|A baseline BMD can still help monitor therapy (p768).":["u215"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): BHOF recommends treatment after hip or vertebral fracture regardless of BMD.":["u137"],"copy|After a distal forearm fracture it recommends treatment with low bone mass and individualization with normal BMD.":["u136"],"copy|BHOF Clinician’s Guide, secondary fracture prevention.":["u138"],"copy|Because of osteosarcoma in rats, the book limits teriparatide to two years (Table 51-5, p774 and prose p776).":["u216"],"copy|Plan an antiresorptive afterward to preserve BMD gains (p776).":["u169"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): The US label allows longer use only if high fracture risk persists or returns.":["u168"],"copy|Forteo prescribing information, §§2.3 and 5.2.":["u170"],"copy|Chronic steroid exposure may suppress the adrenal stress response (Ch 97 p1532).":["u183"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): ACR recommends fracture-risk assessment in adults receiving prednisone-equivalent ≥2.5 mg/day for >3 months.":["u217"],"copy|The ESE/Endocrine Society guidance distinguishes oral stress dosing in minor illness from parenteral coverage when vomiting or hemodynamic instability is present.":["u218"],"copy|ACR glucocorticoid-induced osteoporosis recommendations · ESE/Endocrine Society 2024, recommendation 3.1 and Table 8.":["u185"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): BHOF advises 1,200 mg/day total calcium for women ≥51 and men ≥71, and 1,000 mg/day for men 50–70.":["u80"],"copy|BHOF calcium and vitamin D.":["u219"],"copy|In a separate post-hip-fracture trial, all-cause mortality was 9.6% versus 13% with placebo (pp775–776).":["u83"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): The original post-hip-fracture study reports 13.3% placebo mortality.":["u220"],"copy|HORIZON Recurrent Fracture Trial.":["u221"],"copy|Hazzard states that fracture precedes pain in most cases.":["u222"],"copy|New groin or thigh pain warrants femur radiographs for an atypical femoral fracture; a bone scan or MRI may be needed (pp776–777).":["u94"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): Prodromal thigh or groin pain can precede a complete fracture, as noted in US bisphosphonate prescribing information.":["u94"],"copy|CHAPTER 51 / HAZZARD":["u48"],"copy|Alendronate 70 mg orally weekly reduced hip and vertebral fracture risk by roughly 50% over three years in the studies summarized (Table 51-5, p774). The age subgroups ≤75 and >75 had 51% and 38% lower vertebral fracture risk at 12 months, respectively. Estimated time to benefit in those ≥70 was 8 months (p775). Table 51-5 gives eGFR <35 mL/min as a contraindication (p774).Current practice (not the book): US labeling does not recommend alendronate at creatinine clearance <35 mL/min. Contraindications to oral use include delayed esophageal emptying, inability to remain upright for 30 minutes and hypocalcemia (FDA Fosamax prescribing information).":["u34"],"copy|Teriparatide 20 micrograms SC daily reduced vertebral and nonvertebral fractures by 65% and 53%, respectively, in the trial (pp774,776). Table 51-5 limits therapy to 24 months because of the osteosarcoma signal in rats; p776 likewise says no longer than two years.Current practice (not the book): US labeling permits treatment beyond two years only if high fracture risk persists or returns. Avoid in people at increased baseline osteosarcoma risk, including Paget disease, bone metastases, prior skeletal malignancy or radiation involving the skeleton. Avoid in underlying hypercalcemic disorders as a separate precaution (FDA Forteo prescribing information). Forteo prescribing information, §§2.3 and 5.2.":["u38"],"osteoporosis|Osteoporosis":["u223"],"bone-strength-and-fracture-risk|Bone strength and fracture risk":["u224"],"when-a-fracture-