:["u243"],"copy|Trabecular bone has greater metabolic activity.":["u244"],"copy|Age, prior fracture, gait and strength help explain why people with the same T-score have different risks.":["u192"],"copy|Standard FRAX estimates 10-year fracture probability but omits fall history.":["u193"],"copy|Use a falls history and a brief functional examination alongside DXA.":["u194"],"copy|A person who needs their arms to rise from a chair may have a remediable fall risk.":["u195"],"copy|Post-fracture physical therapy can address strength, balance and body mechanics.":["u196"],"copy|In residential care, the ability to swallow and follow a dosing schedule, renal function and the facility's ability to maintain treatment determine whether a chosen regimen is practical (pp769–772, 778).":["u197"],"copy|Postmenopausal osteoporosis: women 51–75 years, driven by estrogen loss.":["u245"],"copy|Senile osteoporosis: >60 years, both sexes, from reduced bone turnover/osteoblast numbers (p759).":["u246"],"copy|After an unexpected compression fracture or markedly low density, ask what can be reversed.":["u171"],"copy|Excess thyroid hormone accelerates remodeling, so the hyperthyroidism row pairs the diagnosis with TSH and thyroxine.":["u172"],"copy|Hyperparathyroidism points instead to calcium and PTH, myeloma to a blood count and protein electrophoresis, and androgen deprivation or hypogonadism to the endocrine history in men.":["u173"],"copy|Female sex helps estimate baseline risk.":["u174"],"copy|It is not a secondary illness.":["u175"],"copy|Distinguish a brief prednisone burst from cumulative exposure, and remember that type 2 diabetes may raise fracture risk even with relatively preserved BMD (pp768–769, Table 51-3).":["u176"],"copy|Prednisone can reduce osteoblast activity and increase osteoclast-mediated resorption, with bone loss often beginning within six months of starting therapy.":["u177"],"copy|The chapter describes an association with ≥7.5 mg/day for more than three months.":