py|Table 51-5 lists approximately 57% lower vertebral fracture risk with abaloparatide 80 μg SC daily and a maximum duration of 24 months (p774). [sic — the chapter’s 18-month trial account gives an 86% reduction in new vertebral fractures and 43% in nonvertebral fractures (p777).]":["u273"],"copy|Current practice: Abaloparatide duration.":["u274"],"copy|US labeling does not recommend treatment for more than two years in a lifetime.":["u275"],"copy|Tymlos prescribing information.":["u276"],"copy|Bisphosphonates are retained in bone and can still exert an effect after treatment stops.":["u151"],"copy|Consider a patient with no previous fracture and a hip T-score above −2.5 after seven years of alendronate.":["u277"],"copy|A monitored holiday can be considered if current risk is modest.":["u153"],"copy|Reassess age, falls, interval fractures and BMD before pausing.":["u154"],"copy|Reassess during the pause because a new fracture or substantial density loss can change the decision.":["u155"],"copy|Patients with high ongoing risk may benefit from continuing longer (pp776–777).":["u156"],"copy|Romosozumab 210 mg SC monthly for 12 doses.":["u278"],"copy|In FRAME, romosozumab versus placebo for one year, followed by denosumab in both groups, reduced new vertebral fractures 75% at two years.":["u279"],"copy|In ARCH, romosozumab versus alendronate for one year, followed by alendronate in both groups, reduced vertebral, nonvertebral and hip fractures by 48%, 19% and 38%, respectively (p777).":["u280"],"copy|Current practice: Romosozumab and cardiovascular risk.":["u281"],"copy|The anabolic effect wanes over the course.":["u282"],"copy|Follow treatment with an antiresorptive when indicated.":["u283"],"copy|US labeling advises against starting within one year of MI or stroke and calls for individual cardiovascular risk assessment (FDA Evenity boxed warning).":["u284"],"copy|Evenity prescribing information.":["u285"],"copy|New thigh or groin pain after several years of bisphosphonate exposure warrants femur radiographs for a possible incomplete atypical femoral fracture.":["u157"],"copy|Look for a transverse or short oblique lesion of the lateral cortex in the subtrochanteric region or shaft, sometimes with local cortical thickening.":["u158"],"copy|Symptoms can precede a complete fracture.":["u159"],"copy|If plain films are unrevealing and suspicion persists, MRI may identify a stress injury.":["u160"],"copy|The absolute risk is low compared with the number of usual fragility fractures prevented (pp776–777).":["u161"],"copy|AFF: 3.2–50 per 100,000 person-years on bisphosphonates; >100 per 100,000 with long-term use (p777).":["u286"],"copy|Current practice: Prodromal thigh pain.":["u287"],"copy|FDA bisphosphonate labeling notes that prodromal thigh or groin pain may occur weeks or months before a complete fracture.":["u288"],"copy|Alendronate prescribing information.":["u289"],"copy|Raloxifene's demonstrated fracture benefit is vertebral.":["u162"],"copy|Zoledronic acid can cause a transient flu-like illness after infusion.":["u163"],"copy|Jaw osteonecrosis is rare with osteoporosis doses and is a recognized complication of denosumab as well.":["u164"],"copy|Think of exposed jaw bone that has failed to heal for eight weeks, especially after an extraction.":["u165"],"copy|Teriparatide gives a daily, intermittent PTH signal that favors bone formation.":["u166"],"copy|Zoledronic acid 5 mg IV yearly for three years reduced vertebral, nonvertebral and hip fracture risk by 70%, 25% and 41%, respectively, in the postmenopausal osteoporosis trial (p775).":["u290"],"copy|A separate trial in patients treated after hip-fracture repair reported deaths in 9.6% with zoledronic acid versus 13% with placebo (p776).":["u291"],"copy|Current practice: Zoledronic acid trial results.":["u292"],"copy|HORIZON reported the unrounded placebo mortality of 13.3% versus 9.6% with zoledronic acid.":["u293"],"copy|US labeling contraindicates the osteoporosis infusion i